  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1154 PM MDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nMonsoon moisture looks to remain in play through midweek. Quite\na bit moisture will be overhead through most of the day, which\nwill limit thunderstorm potential to mostly areas east of I-15\nand isolated in nature. We COULD a few strikes elsewhere\nespecially if we can get a bit more sunshine this afternoon.\nEven if we don't see a lot of thunderstorms around, virga will\ncertainly be a potential issue. Gusts may stay under 35 mph\nthough, as the potential for gusts over 35 mph is limited to\n10-20% for today. For Tuesday, shower and thunderstorm potential\nis current expected across the South Hill/Albion Mountains and\nsoutheast highlands. More isolated potential exists north of\nIdaho Falls toward the Montana border, as well as in the\ncorridor from Willow Creek Summit to Mackay northeast into\nsouthern Montana. Currently, we are expected less morning cloud\ncover and more instability, indicated by an up to 60% chance of\ngusts over 35 mph. Our last better chance of showers and storms\nwill be Wednesday, as a better surge of monsoon moisture impacts\nthe state. With the potential for more cloud cover at times,\ntemperatures will be a bit cooler than the past few days with\nhighs in the mid 80s to mid 90s. We may end up cooler than that\nfor two reasons: any particular day with more early cloud cover\nAND dense smoke due to fires in Oregon and southwest Idaho. We\nare already seeing that this morning especially from Pocatello\nwest toward Boise. This will be something to watch as dense\nsmoke itself may be enough to quell instability and better\nchances of showers and storms overall.  \n\nStarting Thursday, the ridge shifts back to the west and\npotentially southwest. This should cut off the monsoon moisture\nfor the most part. We could a see a storm or two, but the\npattern is not ideal for continued deeper moisture pushing into\nIdaho. This will push us back toward hotter and drier\nconditions. By the weekend, highs will likely be pushing 100\nagain in several areas and increasing HeatRisk impacts across\neastern Idaho.",
